type {\n   194→            display: inline-block;\n   195→            padding: 2px 6px;\n   196→            font-size: 8pt;\n   197→            border-radius: 2px;\n   198→            margin-right: 4px;\n   199→            font-weight: bold;\n   200→        }\n   201→\n   202→        .update-type.announcement {\n   203→            background-color: #ffe0e0;\n   204→            color: #cc0000;\n   205→        }\n   206→\n   207→        .update-type.feature {\n   208→            background-color: #e0ffe0;\n   209→            color: #008800;\n   210→        }\n   211→\n   212→        .update-type.release {\n   213→            background-color: #e0f0ff;\n   214→            color: #0066cc;\n   215→        }\n   216→\n   217→        footer {\n   218→            background-color: #f6f6ef;\n   219→            padding: 15px 10px;\n   220→            border-top: 1px solid #d4d4d4;\n   221→            text-align: center;\n   222→            font-size: 9pt;\n   223→            color: #828282;\n   224→        }\n   225→\n   226→        footer a {\n   227→            color: #828282;\n   228→            text-decoration: none;\n   229→        }\n   230→\n   231→        footer a:hover {\n   232→            text-decoration: underline;\n   233→        }\n   234→\n   235→        .no-insights {\n   236→            font-style: italic;\n   237→            color: #828282;\n   238→            text-align: center;\n   239→            padding: 20px;\n   240→        }\n   241→\n   242→        @media print {\n   243→            body {\n   244→                background-color: #ffffff;\n   245→            }\n   246→\n   247→            .column {\n   248→                break-inside: avoid;\n   249→            }\n   250→\n   251→            header {\n   252→                page-break-after: avoid;\n   253→            }\n   254→        }\n   255→\n   256→        .source-link {\n   257→            color: #ff6600;\n   258→            text-decoration: none;\n   259→            font-size: 8pt;\n   260→        }\n   261→\n   262→        .source-link:hover {\n   263→            text-decoration: underline;\n   264→        }\n   265→    </style>\n   266→</head>\n   267→<body>\n   268→    <header>\n   269→        <div class=\"container\">\n   270→            <h1>Agentic Coding Research Report</h1>\n   271→            <div class=\"date\">June 18, 2026</div>\n   272→        </div>\n   273→    </header>\n   274→\n   275→    <div class=\"subtitle\">\n   276→        <div class=\"container\">\n   277→            <a href=\"#\">new</a> | <a href=\"#\">past</a> | <a href=\"#\">comments</a> | <a href=\"#\">ask</a> | <a href=\"#\">show</a> | <a href=\"#\">jobs</a> | <a href=\"#\">submit</a>\n   278→        </div>\n   279→    </div>\n   280→\n   281→    <main class=\"container\">\n   282→        <div class=\"three-column-layout\">\n   283→            <!-- New Products Column (40%) -->\n   284→            <section class=\"column new-products\">\n   285→                <div class=\"column-header\">New Products</div>\n   286→                <div class=\"column-content\">\n   287→\n   288→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   289→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   290→                            <a href=\"https://www.zot.sh/\" target=\"_blank\">Zot</a>\n   291→                        </div>\n   292→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   293→                            A lightweight, single-binary coding agent harness written in Go. Works with 20+ AI providers including Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, and local models. Features include four built-in tools (read, write, edit, bash), extensions via subprocess + JSON-RPC, swarm mode for background subagents, and Telegram bridge.\n   294→                        </div>\n   295→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   296→                            <span class=\"category\">AI coding CLI</span> •\n   297→                            <span class=\"tag\">GitHub</span>\n   298→                            <span class=\"tag\">Show HN</span>\n   299→                            <span class=\"tag\">Hacker News</span>\n   300→                            <span>Released: 2026-06-08</span>\n   301→                        </div>\n   302→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   303→                            \"Yet another coding agent harness, but the single-binary approach with no runtime/Docker requirements is refreshing\"\n   304→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Community feedback</span>\n   305→                        </div>\n   306→                    </div>\n   307→\n   308→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   309→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   310→                            <a href=\"https://github.com/OpenYabby/OpenYabby\" target=\"_blank\">OpenYabby</a>\n   311→                        </div>\n   312→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   313→                            Open-source voice-driven multi-agent assistant for macOS. Combines OpenAI Realtime API for voice with CLI runners (Claude Code, Codex, Aider, etc.) to execute real tasks. Features hierarchical multi-agent orchestration, persistent memory with Mem0 + Qdrant, 30+ connectors, and multi-channel support including WhatsApp-native agent threads.\n   314→                        </div>\n   315→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   316→                            <span class=\"category\">Multi-agent orchestration</span> •\n   317→                            <span class=\"tag\">GitHub</span>\n   318→                            <span class=\"tag\">Hacker News</span>\n   319→                            <span class=\"tag\">Product Hunt</span>\n   320→                            <span>Released: 2026-06-01</span>\n   321→                        </div>\n   322→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   323→                            \"The WhatsApp-native agent threads feature is wild - spawn agents and talk to them directly in dedicated chats\"\n   324→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Community feedback</span>\n   325→                        </div>\n   326→                    </div>\n   327→\n   328→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   329→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   330→                            <a href=\"https://deno.com/blog/clawpatrol\" target=\"_blank\">Claw Patrol</a>\n   331→                        </div>\n   332→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   333→                            Open-source security firewall for AI agents released by Deno. Sits between agents and production systems to parse and gate HTTP, SQL, Kubernetes, and SSH protocols. Addresses the critical security need of limiting AI agent access to sensitive data and systems.\n   334→                        </div>\n   335→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   336→                            <span class=\"category\">Security / DevOps agent</span> •\n   337→                            <span class=\"tag\">Deno Blog</span>\n   338→                            <span class=\"tag\">Twitter/X</span>\n   339→                            <span class=\"tag\">Tech media</span>\n   340→                            <span>Released: 2026-05-21</span>\n   341→                        </div>\n   342→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   343→                            \"Deno releasing Claw Patrol as an open-source agent firewall is exactly what the ecosystem needed\"\n   344→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Community feedback</span>\n   345→                        </div>\n   346→                    </div>\n   347→\n   348→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   349→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   350→                            <a href=\"https://www.producthunt.com/products/vexi\" target=\"_blank\">VEXI</a>\n   351→                        </div>\n   352→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   353→                            Open-source AI coding agent for the terminal with Product Hunt launch on June 15, 2026. Bring-your-own-api-key model supporting OpenAI, Anthropic, and other providers. Built specifically for developers who prefer terminal-based workflows.\n   354→                        </div>\n   355→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   356→                            <span class=\"category\">AI coding CLI</span> •\n   357→                            <span class=\"tag\">Product Hunt</span>\n   358→                            <span>Released: 2026-06-15</span>\n   359→                        </div>\n   360→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   361→                            \"Terminal-based approach appeals to developers who live in the CLI\"\n   362→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Community feedback</span>\n   363→                        </div>\n   364→                    </div>\n   365→\n   366→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   367→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   368→                            <a href=\"https://github.com/miracodeai/mira\" target=\"_blank\">Mira</a>\n   369→                        </div>\n   370→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   371→                            Self-hosted AI code reviewer providing an open-source alternative to CodeRabbit and Greptile. Reviews pull requests using any LLM via LiteLLM with low-noise filtering including confidence thresholds, deduplication, and severity sorting. Apache 2.0 licensed.\n   372→                        </div>\n   373→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   374→                            <span class=\"category\">Code review agent</span> •\n   375→                            <span class=\"tag\">GitHub</span>\n   376→                            <span class=\"tag\">Reddit r/SelfHostedAI</span>\n   377→                            <span>Released: 2026-06-10</span>\n   378→                        </div>\n   379→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   380→                            \"Open-source alternative to expensive SaaS code reviewers is exactly what teams want\"\n   381→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Community feedback</span>\n   382→                        </div>\n   383→                    </div>\n   384→\n   385→                </div>\n   386→            </section>\n   387→\n   388→            <!-- Whitelist Updates Column (35%) -->\n   389→            <section class=\"column whitelist-updates\">\n   390→                <div class=\"column-header\">Whitelist Updates</div>\n   391→                <div class=\"column-content\">\n   392→\n   393→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   394→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   395→                            <a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12138966-release-notes\" target=\"_blank\">Claude Fable 5 and Mythos 5 Suspension</a>\n   396→                        </div>\n   397→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   398→                            <span class=\"update-type announcement\">Announcement</span>\n   399→                            <span class=\"tag\">Claude Code</span>\n   400→                            <span>2026-06-12</span>\n   401→                        </div>\n   402→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   403→                            On June 12, 2026, Anthropic suspended access to Claude Fable 5 and Mythos 5 models worldwide following a US export-control directive citing security concerns and a jailbreak vulnerability. Earlier on June 9, Claude had launched Fable 5 as a Mythos-class model made safe for general use. Enterprise plans also gained admin permission management with custom roles on June 2.\n   404→                        </div>\n   405→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   406→                            \"These models were incremental improvements, not doomsday devices\"\n   407→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Hacker News (neutral)</span>\n   408→                        </div>\n   409→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   410→                            \"A very dangerous signal to the rest of the AI industry\"\n   411→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Instagram (negative)</span>\n   412→                        </div>\n   413→                    </div>\n   414→\n   415→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   416→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   417→                            <a href=\"https://docs.devin.ai/release-notes/overview\" target=\"_blank\">Session Folders and Integration Updates</a>\n   418→                        </div>\n   419→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   420→                            <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   421→                            <span class=\"tag\">Devin</span>\n   422→                            <span>2026-06-12</span>\n   423→                        </div>\n   424→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   425→                            Devin introduced Session Folders for organizing sessions into named folders via drag-and-drop. Added !ultra and !fast mid-session toggles for Slack integration. Released Devin Local agent version 2026.5.26 with new file awareness capabilities.\n   426→                        </div>\n   427→                    </div>\n   428→\n   429→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   430→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   431→                            <a href=\"https://cursor.com/changelog\" target=\"_blank\">Bugbot Performance Improvements and SDK Updates</a>\n   432→                        </div>\n   433→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   434→                            <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   435→                            <span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span>\n   436→                            <span>2026-06-10</span>\n   437→                        </div>\n   438→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   439→                            Cursor announced Bugbot is now 3x faster, 22% cheaper, and finds 10% more bugs per review. Average review time reduced from ~5 minutes to ~90 seconds. Introduced Design Mode improvements in browsers and canvases, new SDK functionality with custom tools and auto-review, and Organizations management for Enterprise customers.\n   440→                        </div>\n   441→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   442→                            \"The latency between error detection and correction is collapsing to zero. Moving from code review to state verification\"\n   443→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">LinkedIn (positive)</span>\n   444→                        </div>\n   445→                    </div>\n   446→\n   447→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   448→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   449→                            <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-06-03-github-copilot-in-visual-studio-code-may-releases/\" target=\"_blank\">May Releases (v1.120-v1.123) for VS Code</a>\n   450→                        </div>\n   451→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   452→                            <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   453→                            <span class=\"tag\">GitHub Copilot</span>\n   454→                            <span>2026-06-03</span>\n   455→                        </div>\n   456→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   457→                            GitHub shipped versions v1.120 through v1.123 throughout May and early June 2026. Key features include Agents window in Stable (preview), remote agents support via SSH/Dev Tunnels, air-gapped BYOK models, expanded terminal output compression for tests and builds, and integrated browser with device emulation and screenshot capabilities.\n   458→                        </div>\n   459→                    </div>\n   460→\n   461→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   462→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   463→                            <a href=\"https://geminicli.com/docs/changelogs/\" target=\"_blank\">Version v0.45.0 Stable Release</a>\n   464→                        </div>\n   465→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   466→                            <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   467→                            <span class=\"tag\">Gemini CLI</span>\n   468→                            <span>2026-06-03</span>\n   469→                        </div>\n   470→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   471→                            Released June 3, 2026. Updated default auto-routing and bypassed classifiers 5.0. Resolved critical issues including terminal interactions, Termux relaunch loops, and PTY resize errors. This stable release is recommended for most users.\n   472→                        </div>\n   473→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   474→                            \"How is Gemini CLI still so far behind?\"\n   475→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Reddit (negative)</span>\n   476→                        </div>\n   477→                        <div class=\"quote\">\n   478→                            \"Terrible UI/UX experience and quality of responses being unusable\"\n   479→                            <span class=\"quote-source\">Google AI Developers Forum (negative)</span>\n   480→                        </div>\n   481→                    </div>\n   482→\n   483→                </div>\n   484→            </section>\n   485→\n   486→            <!-- Technical Insights Column (25%) -->\n   487→            <section class=\"column technical-insights\">\n   488→                <div class=\"column-header\">Technical Insights</div>\n   489→                <div class=\"column-content\">\n   490→                    <div class=\"no-insights\">\n   491→                        No technical insights available for this time period.\n   492→                        <br><br>\n   493→                        Insights are collected from thought leaders, tech media, and community sources over the past 7 days.\n   494→                        <br><br>\n   495→                        Check back soon for the latest technical insights and opinions on agentic coding.\n   496→                    </div>\n   497→                </div>\n   498→            </section>\n   499→        </div>\n   500→    </main>\n   501→\n   502→    <footer>\n   503→        <div class=\"container\">\n   504→            <a href=\"#\">Guidelines</a> |\n   505→            <a href=\"#\">FAQ</a> |\n   506→            <a href=\"#\">Lists</a> |\n   507→            <a href=\"#\">API</a> |\n   508→            <a href=\"#\">Security</a> |\n   509→            <a href=\"#\">Legal</a> |\n   510→            <a href=\"#\">Apply to YC</a> |\n   511→            <a href=\"#\">Contact</a>\n   512→            <br><br>\n   513→            <span>Search: <a href=\"#\">Agentic Coding Research Report</a></span>\n   514→        </div>\n   515→    </footer>\n   516→</body>\n   517→</html>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
  },
